WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:14.663
Hello, It's Alex here.

2
00:00:15.443 --> 00:00:20.765
I'm at the library right now researching one of the world landmarks we learned about this year in

3
00:00:20.805 --> 00:00:21.965
Mr. Fay's class.

4
00:00:22.885 --> 00:00:27.706
Mr. Fay gave everyone in the class an object that represents a landmark.

5
00:00:28.727 --> 00:00:33.068
I got this one: a mini ship filled with cargo containers.

6
00:00:33.728 --> 00:00:36.450
Can you guess which world landmark it represents?

7
00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:38.551
That's right!

8
00:00:39.411 --> 00:00:47.055
This mini ship represents the Panama Canal, an international waterway that is also one of the seven modern

9
00:00:47.155 --> 00:00:48.316
wonders of the world.

10
00:00:49.357 --> 00:00:55.960
The canal is located in central Panama, which is the southernmost country in Central America.

11
00:00:56.941 --> 00:01:02.984
The country itself is on an isthmus—a strip of land connecting two larger pieces of land.

12
00:01:04.008 --> 00:01:08.212
Mr. Fay told us that Panama connects North and South America.

13
00:01:09.173 --> 00:01:16.079
But here is what the country is most famous for: its east coast borders the Atlantic Ocean, and

14
00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:18.802
its west coast borders the Pacific Ocean.

15
00:01:19.142 --> 00:01:23.085
You're probably wondering why Panama's location is such a big deal.

16
00:01:24.026 --> 00:01:30.092
Well, here's why it eventually became really important to sailors and traders around the world.

17
00:01:31.720 --> 00:01:36.644
Remember the European explorers who arrived in the Americas in the 1500s?

18
00:01:37.644 --> 00:01:45.290
Mr. Fay said that their explorations led to settlements and the start of many new international trade routes.

19
00:01:46.271 --> 00:01:52.295
But sailors who wanted to cross from the Atlantic to the Pacific Ocean had to travel along the

20
00:01:52.415 --> 00:01:56.158
southern end of South America to get around the continent.

21
00:01:57.179 --> 00:02:05.558
This journey took months, and sailors had to navigate around fierce winds, floating icebergs, and rocky shores.

22
00:02:06.558 --> 00:02:08.939
Many sailors did not survive the trip.

23
00:02:09.899 --> 00:02:15.781
For hundreds of years, people had been looking for an easier way to travel from the Atlantic to

24
00:02:15.801 --> 00:02:16.481
the Pacific.

25
00:02:17.321 --> 00:02:21.762
People tried creating land routes through Panama, including a railroad.

26
00:02:23.483 --> 00:02:28.084
Finally, in 1880, the French decided to dig a canal.

27
00:02:29.052 --> 00:02:36.415
Led by Ferdinand de Lesseps, the builder of the Suez Canal, the French planned to dig a sea-level

28
00:02:36.475 --> 00:02:41.337
canal. But digging that canal proved to be nearly impossible.

29
00:02:42.217 --> 00:02:48.320
Panama has a long rainy season, and the land was covered with dense, tropical jungles.

30
00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:55.903
Then there was the mighty Chagres River, which wound its way through Panama with a wild flow that

31
00:02:55.963 --> 00:02:58.644
grew even more unpredictable when it rained.

32
00:02:59.688 --> 00:03:05.630
Mr. Fay showed us a slide show of the dirty, tired workers who dug and blasted the earth

33
00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:06.870
all day long.

34
00:03:07.790 --> 00:03:14.252
Mosquitoes were everywhere, spreading devastating diseases like malaria and yellow fever.

35
00:03:15.292 --> 00:03:18.413
Mudslides from the rain were a constant problem.

36
00:03:20.074 --> 00:03:28.176
Finally, after twenty thousand workers had died, and investors had lost lots of money, the French decided to

37
00:03:28.236 --> 00:03:28.956
stop digging.

38
00:03:30.044 --> 00:03:35.707
But American President Theodore Roosevelt wasn't ready to give up on the project yet.

39
00:03:36.728 --> 00:03:43.492
He saw the canal as vitally important to his country, a way for America to gain prosperity through

40
00:03:43.592 --> 00:03:44.912
international trading.

41
00:03:45.813 --> 00:03:48.875
In 1904 the American effort began.

42
00:03:49.835 --> 00:03:54.298
Of course, the Americans faced all the problems that had defeated the French.

43
00:03:55.298 --> 00:04:03.557
But the Americans quickly realized something important: there was no way to complete the project without creating healthy

44
00:04:03.617 --> 00:04:05.018
conditions for the workers.

45
00:04:06.058 --> 00:04:14.801
So they made this a priority, sanitizing workers' living spaces and spraying pesticides to kill the disease-carrying mosquitoes.

46
00:04:15.901 --> 00:04:21.282
Over five thousand workers died, but the death toll could have been much higher.

47
00:04:22.843 --> 00:04:27.024
The Americans also made a huge change in the canal's engineering.

48
00:04:27.952 --> 00:04:34.135
Instead of digging one that was sea level, they decided to construct a "lake-and-lock" canal.

49
00:04:35.275 --> 00:04:42.158
Most of the kids in our class—including me—had no idea what a "lake-and-lock" canal is.

50
00:04:43.179 --> 00:04:48.821
Mr. Fay explained that a lock is part of a canal with a gate at each end.

51
00:04:49.822 --> 00:04:54.484
The gates can be opened and closed to control the canal's water level.

52
00:04:55.476 --> 00:05:01.438
The Americans' idea was to dam the Chagres River to create a huge lake.

53
00:05:02.578 --> 00:05:08.780
Then a series of locks could be used to raise ships from the Atlantic Ocean to the lake's

54
00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:16.022
level. The ships could cross the lake, and drop down again through another set of locks to reach

55
00:05:16.082 --> 00:05:17.102
the Pacific Ocean.

56
00:05:18.662 --> 00:05:24.164
If you ask me, using locks in the Panama Canal was an ingenious idea.

57
00:05:25.244 --> 00:05:28.227
But Americans weren't the only ones who had thought of it.

58
00:05:29.147 --> 00:05:35.032
A French engineer had proposed the same thing about thirty years earlier, at the same meeting where de

59
00:05:35.072 --> 00:05:38.435
Lesseps proposed his sea-level canal.

60
00:05:38.615 --> 00:05:45.541
In 1914, when the Panama Canal opened at last, the locks system worked flawlessly!

61
00:05:46.642 --> 00:05:55.129
About 82 kilometers long, the waterway finally provided safe passage for ships from the Atlantic to the Pacific.

62
00:05:56.090 --> 00:06:03.616
In the canal's first year, one thousand ships traveled through it—international shipping and trade would never be the

63
00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:04.416
same again!

64
00:06:06.058 --> 00:06:14.945
Today about 14,000 ships and over 300 million tons of cargo pass through the canal each year.

65
00:06:16.006 --> 00:06:22.611
I had to research what is in those long, colorful shipping containers, and here's what I learned: oil,

66
00:06:23.011 --> 00:06:27.259
grains, metals, and coal make up a lot of the cargo.

67
00:06:28.279 --> 00:06:31.642
Bananas and automobiles are often in the containers too.

68
00:06:32.603 --> 00:06:39.388
It's not just cargo ships traveling along the canal; you can take the 15-40 hour trip aboard a

69
00:06:39.468 --> 00:06:40.669
luxury cruise ship.

70
00:06:41.490 --> 00:06:47.734
The amazing brains of the engineers who dreamed up the canal, along with the heroic work of over

71
00:06:47.955 --> 00:06:54.284
eighty thousand laborers, helped to make the Panama Canal one of the modern wonders of the world.

72
00:06:55.225 --> 00:06:59.248
Mr. Fay called it "a triumph of man over nature."

73
00:07:00.830 --> 00:07:03.492
And there are plans to change the canal again.

74
00:07:04.413 --> 00:07:11.138
A seven-year expansion plan that includes adding more locks was launched in 2007.

75
00:07:12.099 --> 00:07:15.342
It will allow even more ships to pass through the canal.

76
00:07:17.063 --> 00:07:24.100
Whew! I feel like someone opened the locks on me and flooded my brain with too much information!

77
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:28.402
The library will be closing soon, so I should get going.

78
00:07:29.323 --> 00:07:35.046
But one last thing: President Roosevelt never even got to see the finished canal.

79
00:07:35.846 --> 00:07:36.787
Isn't that sad?

80
00:07:38.088 --> 00:07:40.129
I guess being the president of the U.S.

81
00:07:40.209 --> 00:07:42.690
is a big job, and he was really busy.

82
00:07:43.691 --> 00:07:45.472
See you soon, Little Fox readers!
